\section{Lower bounds}{


With lower bounds the fit is the mode over the coefficients at or above their bounds: a coefficient that would
go below its bound stays at it (a bound of 0 keeps coefficients non-negative). Bounded coefficients need a
\code{"none"}, \code{"normal"}, \code{"laplace"} or \code{"elasticnet"} prior. The fit runs coordinate descent:
\code{algorithm = "auto"} does not take Newton steps when a free coefficient has a bound, and
\code{algorithm = "newton"} is an error. Convergence tests are one-sided at a bound: a coefficient at its bound
is optimal when the gradient of the penalized objective does not point below it. Under the \code{"gap"}
criterion, Laplace logistic fits are certified by the duality gap and fits with a normal part on every free
coefficient by the strong-convexity bound; others stop uncertified, on the \code{"kkt"} test of coordinate descent
(logistic regression) or its estimate of the remaining decrease. The reported
log prior is that of the prior without the bounds. A start below a bound starts at the bound.
}

\section{Prior types}{


We specify all priors in terms of their variance parameters.
Similar fitting tools for regularized regression often parameterize the Laplace distribution
in terms of a rate \code{"lambda"} per observation.
See \code{"glmnet"}, for example.

variance = 2 * / (nobs * lambda)^2 or lambda = sqrt(2 / variance) / nobs

The \code{"elasticnet"} prior penalizes each coefficient by
\code{lambda * (laplaceFraction * abs(beta) + (1 - laplaceFraction) / (2 * s) * beta^2)}, with
\code{lambda = sqrt(2 / variance)} as for the Laplace prior, and \code{s} the standard deviation of the outcome
(with divisor \code{nobs}) for \code{"ls"} models and 1 for other models, so that \code{"ls"} fits do not
depend on the units of the outcome. A \code{laplaceFraction} of 1 gives the Laplace prior and 0 a normal prior
with variance \code{s / lambda}. Cross-validation chooses the variance for a fixed \code{laplaceFraction}, as
\code{cv.glmnet} chooses \code{lambda} for a fixed \code{alpha}. The \code{glmnet} fit with
\code{standardize = FALSE} and the same \code{alpha} has \code{lambda = sqrt(2 / variance) / nobs} for
\code{"lr"} models and \code{sqrt(2 / variance) / (2 * nobs)} for \code{"ls"} models.
}

